Washington, DC — Representative Gregory W. Meeks, Ranking Member of the House Foreign Affairs Committee, sent letters to the agencies under the Committee’s jurisdiction, warning them to preserve all records and not destroy them to avoid congressional scrutiny.

Ranking Member Meeks sent letters to:

The Department of State;

the U.S. Agency for International Development (USAID);

the U.S. Agency for Global Media (USAGM);

the U.S. International Development Finance Corporation (DFC);

the Millennium Challenge Corporation (MCC);

the Peace Corps;

and the U.S. Trade and Development Agency (USTDA).

Washington, D.C. — Today, Rep. Robert Garcia, Ranking Member of the House Committee on Oversight and Government Reform, released document preservation letters laying the groundwork to advance investigations led by Oversight Democrats related to Trump family profiteering that may threaten the public interest, corporate corruption, misconduct at the Department of Homeland Security (DHS), and the ongoing White House cover-up of the Jeffrey Epstein files. These investigations will provide the facts, evidence, and insights necessary to identify gaps in existing law, inform legislative solutions, and advance meaningful reforms that better protect the American people.

“Oversight Democrats are fighting to put an end to this Administration’s self-dealing, misconduct, and abuse. These investigations will lay the groundwork for legislation which helps all Americans. This is just the beginning,” said Ranking Member Robert Garcia.

Ranking Member Robert Garcia escalated existing investigations initiated by Oversight Democrats this Congress and demanded the preservation of all records, communications, and information pertaining to future congressional probes.

Ranking Member Garcia sent the following 12 document retention notices related to the following individuals surrounding Trump family corruption, corporate corruption, DHS misconduct, and the White House Epstein cover-up:

Mr. Donald Trump, Jr. regarding alleged conflicts of interest, his stake in World Liberty Financial, and the financing of his ‘Red Wedding’ by a Russian oligarch;

Mr. Eric Trump regarding alleged conflicts of interest and his stake in World Liberty Financial;

Mr. Jared Kushner regarding alleged conflicts of interest, his control of and stake in Affinity Partners, and allegations of using his role with the government to enrich himself and his companies;

Former Ambassador to Greece Kimberly Guilfoyle regarding alleged pay-to-play schemes surrounding her ambassadorship;

Trust for the National Mall CEO Catherine Townsend on $600 million being drawn from both taxpayer funds and private donors with business before the government, including Nvidia, a top donor which allegedly received favorable regulatory treatment from the Trump Administration;

White House Chief of Staff Stephen Miller regarding DHS and ICE abuses under his direction;

Former DHS Secretary Kristi Noem regarding allegations of abuse of DHS funds, circumventing corruption safeguards, and enriching herself and her associates through her official position while overseeing DHS and ICE abuses;

Former DHS Senior Advisor Corey Lewandowski regarding allegations of corruption and self-dealing, using access to Kristi Noem to “carry out private business,” steering lucrative government contracts toward allies, and his role in DHS and ICE abuses;

Vice President J.D. Vance regarding secret meetings inside the White House Situation Room about the Administration’s cover-up of the Epstein files and mitigating the political fallout surrounding the Epstein investigation;

Former White House Chief of Staff Susie Wiles regarding secret meetings inside the White House Situation Room about the Administration’s cover-up of the Epstein files and mitigating the political fallout surrounding the Epstein investigation;

Attorney General Todd Blanche regarding the cover-up of the Epstein files, the Committee’s outstanding subpoena and DOJ’s failure to comply with the Epstein Files Transparency Act (EFTA), DOJ’s botched review and release of documents, and the decision to transfer Ghislaine Maxwell to a minimum-security prison camp; and

Federal Bureau of Prisons Director William K. Marshall III and Federal Prison Camp Bryan Prison Warden Tanisha Hall regarding the transfer of Ghislaine Maxwell to a minimum-security prison camp days after Maxwell offered favorable testimony about President Trump, in addition to the favorable privileges she has enjoyed at the prison camp.

